doc.para:
while statement
　The while statement repeatedly executes a statement or block while a specified condition is true. It allows you to create a loop.
　The syntax is as follows:

while(expression)
	statement or block


　The "statement or block" is repeatedly executed as long as the conditional expression `expression` is true.

　Its operation is as follows:
(1) First, `expression` is evaluated; if it is false, the statement or block is not executed
(2) Execute the statement or block
(3) Evaluate `expression`; if the result is true, go to (2); otherwise, the `while` statement ends

　In other words, if `expression` is false from the beginning, the statement or block is not executed. Also, `expression` is not continuously evaluated; its truth value is checked once every time the statement or block is executed.
　Also, at step (3), if it is a block, it temporarily exits the block (meaning variables and such are cleared).

	while(func()) ;
	/* As long as func() is true, do nothing and keep evaluating func() */

	while(func()) i++;
	/* As long as func() is true, keep executing i++ */

	while(i < 5) // While i is less than 5
	{
		func(i);
		i++;
	}

break
　The break statement is written when you want to terminate the while loop prematurely.

	while(true) // Infinite loop unless break is executed
	{
		if(func()) break; // If func() is true, end while
		func2(); // Comes here if break is not executed above
	}
	// Comes here when break is executed

continue
　The continue statement is written when you want to restart from the evaluation of the conditional expression mid-way.
　When continue is executed, execution moves to (3).

	var i = 0;
	while(i < 5)
	{
		if(func1()) continue;
		// If func1() is true, the condition i < 5 is checked, and if true,
		// execution resumes from the beginning of the block
		func2();
		i++;
	}

do...while The do...while statement is the same as the while statement, but it does not check the conditional expression at the beginning. The syntax is as follows:
do statement or block while(expression);
Because it does not check the conditional expression first, the "statement or block" will be executed even if the conditional expression is false at the point of `do`. Once the "statement or block" is executed, the conditional expression is checked; if false, it exits the loop, and if true, the statement or block is executed again, repeating this process.
